{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act just-cause eviction grounds","requirement":"A landlord may evict a residential tenant only on one of the statutory grounds in the Anti-Eviction Act (e.g., nonpayment, disorderly conduct, lease breach, condo conversion, retiring the property), after the notice the ground requires; relocation assistance must be paid before certain evictions.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Most residential rentals: single-family homes, mobile homes and land in a mobile home park, apartment buildings, complexes, rooming and boarding homes. Exempt: two- or three-unit owner-occupied premises with two or fewer rental units; transient hotel, motel and guest-house occupants.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied","unit_limit":3}],"per_tenancy":"A unit held in trust for a developmentally disabled immediate family member who permanently occupies it is not covered; hotel or motel guests with no alternate residence who reside continually are covered.","coverage_quotes":["The Anti-Eviction Act applies to most residential rental properties including single-family homes, mobile homes and land in a mobile home park, apartment buildings, and complexes.","This law may not apply to two - or three-unit owner-occupied premises with two (2) or fewer rental units."]},"exemptions":"Two- or three-unit owner-occupied premises with two or fewer rental units; hotel, motel and guest house transient or seasonal guests; a unit held in trust for a developmentally disabled immediate family member who permanently occupies it.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"This law may not apply to two - or three-unit owner-occupied premises with two (2) or fewer rental units. It does not apply to hotel guests, motel guests, or guest houses rented to a transient guest or seasonal tenant.","interaction":"Eviction for failure to pay a rent increase requires that the increase comply with other laws and municipal ordinances, including rent control.","relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey self-help eviction and lockout prohibition","requirement":"A landlord may not evict a tenant through self-help—entering without permission, padlocking, changing locks, shutting off utilities or removing belongings—without a court judgment; only a court officer may physically evict after a warrant for removal.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential rental premises in New Jersey; binds a landlord or anyone acting on the landlord's behalf.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["Self-help evictions, or lockouts, made by the landlord are illegal in New Jersey.","A landlord or their agent may not padlock, disconnect utilities or otherwise block entry to a rental premise while the tenant still lives there."]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"A landlord who illegally evicts or holds a tenant's belongings unlawfully may be liable for damages to the tenant; the court may award possession, damages, court costs and reasonable attorney fees, and the landlord may be charged with disorderly conduct.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:39-1","quoted_span":"Self-help evictions, or lockouts, made by the landlord are illegal in New Jersey.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-03","n_rules":2,"note":null}

